    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1">

                <pb facs="0015" n="1" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg1"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.001 Chiang Kai-shek/17: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-01-04T08:00:00+07:00">January 4, 1944—8
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 11:31 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>13. Chiang’s<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1-f1"/> New Year radio address to Chinese Army
                    [and] people (Embassy’s 10, January 4<anchor n="3" xml:id="d1-f2"/>) although
                    made January 1, 1 a.m. was not passed by censors until evening January 2. Mme.
                    Chiang reportedly held up release address due to disapproval English translation
                    but foreign press correspondents believed 48 hours delay must have been due
                    other factors, finally resolved after long discussion, such as (1) lavish praise
                    United States, President Roosevelt in contrast slight mention Great Britain,
                        Churchill<anchor n="4" xml:id="d1-f3"/> and (2) mention Chiang–Roosevelt
                    conversation at Cairo regarding Japan.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d1-f4"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1-f1" n="2" xml:id="d1-f1-content">
                <p>Generalissimo Chiang Kai-shek, President of the National Government of the
                    Republic of China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1-f2" n="3" xml:id="d1-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1-f3" n="4" xml:id="d1-f3-content">
                <p>Winston S. Churchill, British Prime Minister.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1-f4" n="5" xml:id="d1-f4-content">
                <p>For documentation on the Cairo Conference, see <ref target="frus1943CairoTehran">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, The Conferences at Cairo and
                        Tehran, 1943</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
